- **Case typo:** {{serial.begin()}} instead of {{Serial.begin()}}. C++ is case-sensitive.
- **Wrong board or core:** ATtiny85 and similar cores have no hardware UART. Check Tools > Board.
- **Missing header** in a .cpp or PlatformIO file. Add {{#include <Arduino.h>}}.
- **Wrong scope:** {{Serial}} used in a class file that never included Arduino.h.
**Corrected sketch:**
~~~cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);          // capital S; baud must match the monitor
  while (!Serial) { }          // wait for USB-serial (Leonardo/ESP32)
  Serial.println(F("Boot OK"));
}

void loop() { }
~~~
**Verify:** open the Serial Monitor at 9600 baud and confirm "Boot OK". On an ATtiny use {{SoftwareSerial}} on two free pins.`,
`**Nyquist-Shannon theorem:** a band-limited signal with highest frequency {{f_max}} is perfectly reconstructable only if {{fs ≥ 2·f_max}}. {{2·f_max}} is the **Nyquist rate**; {{fs/2}} is the **Nyquist frequency**.
- **Aliasing:** a tone above {{fs/2}} folds back to {{|f − k·fs|}} and is indistinguishable from a real low-frequency tone.
- **Anti-alias filter:** low-pass the analog input below {{fs/2}} before the ADC.
- **Example:** a 7 kHz tone sampled at 10 kHz shows up as a false 3 kHz tone.
~~~c
float aliasFreq(float f, float fs) {
  float r = fmodf(f, fs);
  return (r > fs / 2) ? fs - r : r;   // folded frequency
}
// aliasFreq(7000, 10000) -> 3000 Hz
~~~
**Engineering rule:** sample at 2.5x to 10x f_max in practice (audio uses 44.1 kHz for 20 kHz content) so the filter has room to roll off. Try it on the Lab Sandbox oscilloscope.`,
